title: Comparisons
tags: Query
kind: reference
summary: Comparisons are same-type, numbers form one domain, dates compare by instant; comparing with nothing or ordering a type with no order is refused up front.

A field name has one value type in the repository ([[One value type per field name]]),
so a comparison is always ''same-type'': the operand is compared with rows of the
field's type, with no cross-type rules. `int` and `float` form one numeric domain;
`datetime` compares by its instant (Unix milliseconds). A `nothing` row never matches
a value comparison ([[Presence and absence]]).

A date operand needs the date literal — `mfr_mtime > @"2024-01-01"` or
`@1704067200000`; a plain string would compare against nothing, the field being a
`datetime`. The ISO-8601 forms are those `core::date::iso_to_ms` accepts
(`@"2024-01-01"`, `@"2024-01-01 12:30:00"`), read as UTC; an unparsable one is
refused.

On a `tree_ref` field a string operand names a node: `mfr_path = "/music/jazz"` is
the one node at that path ([[Field aspects]]).

!! Refused before evaluation

Two shapes have no useful meaning and are refused with `400` before anything
evaluates the query — a property of the query, not of what serves it:

* a comparison whose operand is `nothing` — absence is asked with `IS ABSENT` / `IS UNKNOWN`;
* an ''ordered'' comparison (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) on a type with no meaningful order: `bool` and the reference types. Equality on them is fine; strings order lexicographically, numbers and dates numerically.

Other refusals need the field's stored type — a tree-only aspect on another type,
`osm` in path mode on a non-`tree_ref`, a `:path` operand that is not a string —
and are made one step later from the daemon's field catalogue, still before
evaluation. Size limits are checked at the same point ([[Query limits]]).
